Hey Huaxin,

I imagine it must be hard to get into the code. Almost all of the code
is Thorsten Joachims'. Also, my modifications were written in a hurry
and are not very tidy. Sorry! As I said before, I wasn't intending to
release the code and the modifications were only supposed to be for my
use :)

For multiple queries the most relevant part of the code is lines 541 to
812. Currently, for k-at-a-time querying the code looks at the closest
2k images to the current hyperplane and then does a greedy search for
the k images (within the set of 2k images) that are most mutually far
apart from each other. There are probably other ideas that you may be
interested in exploring.

For running experiments here's what I do. You may want to explore
something different if you think it will help. I've spent some time
writting scripts that automate most of the process.


*** Initial data processing:

1) I get the original data file and split it up into four data sets each
consisting of the 600 images. The four data sets correspond to the four
different labelings that the images can take -- architecture, people
etc.
This splitting can be done automatically using my perl script: mdatSplit

2) I normalize each of the features in the data sets to have zero mean
and variance 1. I use my perl script: datNormalize to do this.

*** For each topic I then do the following:

3) I then create an initial training set of one randomly selected
positive and one randomly selected negative image. datCreateActiveTrain
does this.

4) I then run the non-active and simple active svm learning algorithms
on this data set.

5) Next I run the classifying module on the data set and look at the
accuracy that it's achieved.

I've spent some time creating a Perl script that does steps 3) 4) and 5)
automatically: imageActiveRunAllValues.

Usage: imageActiveRunAllValues <topic> <num_train> <num_runs> <kernel
type (1=poly 2=gaussian)> <kernel param> <num_simultaneous queries>
[number initial random queries]

<topic> is the file stem of the desired topic. E.g. if I have a data
file called "people.normalized.dat" then topic is "people.normalized".

<num_train> is the largest number of labeled training examples we want
the svms to see. If we are doing 3 rounds or 20-at-a-time querying then
we set this to 3*20 + 2 = 62. The +2 is because we always see one
positive and one negative image.

<num runs> is the number of repeated experimental runs we want to do.

<kernel param> is the degree of the polynomial kernel if we set kernel
type to polynomials. <kernel param> is the gamma value if we set kernel
type = gaussian.

<num simultaneous queries> is the k value for k-at-a-time querying

I've experimented with creating an inital random dataset first and then
doing active learning on that. [number inital random queries] sets the
size of this random data set.

*** Example run

So if I want to test the birds topic here's what I do. I create a dat
file birds.dat consisting of just the labeling for the birds data set. I
then normalized it to get birds.normalized.dat.

Suppose I want to try a gaussian kernel with gamma = 0.01. Suppose
further that I want to first start out with 20 randomly selected images
and then do 20-at-a-time querying for at most 4 rounds. I want to do
this experiment 25 times.

I then run the script:

imageActiveRunAllValues architecture.normalized 82 25 2 0.01 2 20

The scrpit with then output 4 results files:

architecture.normalized.results.22.txt
architecture.normalized.results.42.txt
architecture.normalized.results.62.txt
architecture.normalized.results.82.txt

These contain the performance results after the SVMs have seen 22, 42,
62 and 82 images respectively; i.e after 1,2,3 and 4 rounds of querying.

Each file contains the average accuracy and standard error of the top-n
returned images for various n. 
Ran = Random querying SVM
SAct = Simple active SVM

Here's the type of results you should be getting after 4 rounds of
querying 20-at-time (architecture.normalized.results.82.txt):


Topic: architecture.normalized
Number of inital random queries: 20
Number of querying rounds: 3
Number of simulanteous queries per round: 20
Number of labeled instances seen: 82
Gamma: 0.01
Number of runs: 25

Ran10      20     30      40      50      60      70      80      90 ...
SAct10     20     30      40      50      60      70      80      90 ...
.
.
.

Mean
99.5    98.75  98.501  98.125  97.9    96.915  96.2845 95.125  93.1115 
...
100     99     98.1675 98.25   98.4    98.499  98.213  97.5    96.946
...

So, for example, the active SVM gets an average of 98.213 percent
accuracy for the 70 images that it is most confident are about
architecture.

Simon.

